      "title": "Passage 1: The History of Photography",
      "section": 1,
      "content": "<p class=\"mb-6\"><strong class=\"font-bold text-lg\">A</strong> The history of photography began long before the first photograph was taken. The camera obscura, a darkened room or box with a small hole that projects an inverted image of the outside scene, was known to ancient Chinese and Greek scholars. Renaissance artists used camera obscuras as drawing aids, but the challenge of permanently capturing the projected image remained unsolved for centuries.</p><p class=\"mb-6\"><strong class=\"font-bold text-lg\">B</strong> The first permanent photograph was produced by French inventor Nicephore Niepce in 1826, using a process he called heliography. The exposure time required was approximately eight hours, making it impractical for anything other than still scenes. It was Niepce's partner Louis Daguerre who developed the first commercially viable photographic process, the daguerreotype, announced to the world in 1839.</p><p class=\"mb-6\"><strong class=\"font-bold text-lg\">C</strong> The 20th century brought colour photography, instant cameras, and eventually digital technology. The first digital camera was built by Kodak engineer Steven Sasson in 1975. It weighed 3.6 kilograms and took 23 seconds to capture a single black-and-white image at a resolution of 0.01 megapixels. The irony was that Kodak, the company that invented digital photography, would eventually be bankrupted by it.</p><p class=\"mb-6\"><strong class=\"font-bold text-lg\">D</strong> Today, an estimated 1.4 trillion photographs are taken annually, the vast majority on smartphones. Photography has become so ubiquitous that it has fundamentally altered how humans experience and remember events. Social media platforms built around photo sharing have created new forms of visual communication.</p>",
